---
// The SVH explainer — drawn from "What Somatic Vocal Healing Is".
// Opens with the one-breath definition, then alternating image+text rows.
// Content splits on pro state: novice blocks (.wn-novice) show by default;
// practitioner blocks (.wn-pro) appear when door 3 is active (.we-site.is-pro).
// Images are props (default to the svhgpt set) so a sibling page can pass real
// photos with their own alt text.
interface Props {
  lostImg?: string;
  lostAlt?: string;
  soundingImg?: string;
  soundingAlt?: string;
}
const {
  lostImg = '/media/library/svhgpt-02-we-lost-the-voice.webp',
  lostAlt = 'A figure with a smaller, sounding self glowing at the heart',
  soundingImg = '/media/library/svhgpt-03-sounding-not-singing.webp',
  soundingAlt = 'A profile sounding, frequency rings rippling from the open mouth',
} = Astro.props;
---

<section class="wn-story" id="breath">
  <div class="wn-story-inner">
    <!-- In one breath — the definition everyone reads -->
    <div class="wn-breath wn-reveal">
      <div class="wn-breath-mark" data-parallax data-parallax-speed="0.22" aria-hidden="true">
        <img src="/brand/symbol-orange.png" alt="" />
      </div>
      <div class="wn-breath-eyebrow">What it is, in one breath</div>
      <p class="wn-breath-head">
        Somatic Vocal Healing is using your own voice to be
        <em>present with what is alive in you</em> — by giving it sound.
        Not a beautiful sound. The <em>honest</em> sound of what is actually there.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="wn-rows">
      <!-- ── Row 1 · NOVICE: we lost the voice (svhgpt-02) ── -->
      <div class="wn-row wn-novice wn-mode wn-reveal">
        <div class="wn-row-media wn-row-media--tall" data-parallax data-parallax-speed="0.16">
          <img src={lostImg} alt={lostAlt} loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="wn-row-text">
          <div class="wn-row-eyebrow">Where it begins</div>
          <h3 class="wn-row-h3">We didn't lose the voice. <em>We just lost track of the keys.</em></h3>
          <p>
            As babies we were true to instinct. Joy became laughter; pain became
            the sound of pain. Nobody taught us — the voice and the feeling
            weren't yet separated.
          </p>
          <p>
            At some point, most of us stopped. Our funerals are silent.
            No meeting pauses to <em>sound</em> its stress. So this work isn't really
            teaching you anything new.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>"I don't have to teach you how to do it. I just have to bring
            your awareness back to what you've never forgotten."</strong> It's as if
            the car is in the garage and you've only misplaced the keys. The work is
            mostly about finding them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Row 1 · PRO: the missing channel (svhgpt-02) ── -->
      <div class="wn-row wn-pro wn-reveal">
        <div class="wn-row-media wn-row-media--tall" data-parallax data-parallax-speed="0.16">
          <img src={lostImg} alt={lostAlt} loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="wn-row-text">
          <div class="wn-row-eyebrow">Where the voice fits</div>
          <h3 class="wn-row-h3">The voice is the <em>missing channel</em> between body and talk.</h3>
          <p>
            Body-oriented work reaches almost everything, because the body stores
            it all — but the body is largely mute, and laying it open to a
            practitioner is deeply vulnerable. Talk routes everything through the
            mind; you have to <em>find</em> your own way to what the body already knows.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sound sits in the middle. The voice <strong>"accesses stuff from the
            body and brings it in a format you can start interpreting and working
            with."</strong> Much of the body's access without the exposure of
            hands-on work; much of the clarity of talk, without putting
            everything into a story.
          </p>
          <p>
            It's its own channel — between the body and the mind, drawing on both.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ── Row 2 · sounding, not singing (svhgpt-03) — shared, pro adds titration ── -->
      <div class="wn-row wn-row--reverse wn-reveal">
        <div class="wn-row-media" data-parallax data-parallax-speed="0.15">
          <img src={soundingImg} alt={soundingAlt} loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="wn-row-text">
          <div class="wn-row-eyebrow">Sounding, not singing</div>
          <h3 class="wn-row-h3">We <em>welcome</em> the crack and the wobble.</h3>
          <p>
            Singing is its own beautiful art — it reaches for a result, something
            that sounds good. <strong>Sounding</strong> is simply a different aim:
            reaching for what's inside that wants to come out and be acknowledged.
            A shaky sound, a rough sound, a sound that fades into nothing — none
            of it is a mistake. It's the body talking.
          </p>
          <p>
            You don't need a good voice. If anything, trained singers carry a small
            disadvantage: the reflex to <em>perform</em> a sound rather than let the
            real one come. A plain, ordinary voice is perfect for this.
          </p>
          <!-- Pro-only deepening: the voice as built-in titration -->
          <div class="wn-note wn-pro">
            <p class="wn-note-q">For somatic practitioners — the voice as built-in titration.</p>
            <p>
              The voice is a wise gatekeeper to the unconscious. It regulates
              <strong>two</strong> things at once: the <em>kind</em> of material that
              surfaces and its <em>intensity</em> — the dose is "always just right,
              like mother's milk. Not too hot, not too cold." You're working
              <em>with</em> a system that already self-doses, not forcing anything open.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
